{
  "image_name": "4b50279936709849a0ea4c25ac9b84bf.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how T-cell activation couples calcium signaling, mitochondrial function, and gene regulation. At the plasma membrane, TCR signaling activates PLCγ1, producing Ins(1,4,5)P3, which triggers calcium release from the ER through InsP3 receptors. ER calcium depletion is then sensed by STIM proteins, which appear to activate ORAI channels and drive store-operated calcium entry from outside the cell.\n\nMitochondria sit close to the ER and membrane channels and take up calcium through the MCU, suggesting they buffer local calcium and integrate these signals. In parallel, mitochondria generate ATP, which is released through pannexin 1 channels at the membrane. Extracellular ATP then acts back on purinergic receptors such as P2RX1, P2RX4, or P2RX7, causing additional calcium influx. The diagram emphasizes a feed-forward loop in which ATP release and purinergic signaling reinforce calcium entry.\n\nThese calcium signals converge on the nucleus, where transcription factors including NFAT and RORγt are activated, while FOXP3 is shown as being negatively affected by this pathway. The downstream outcome appears to favor expression of glycolytic enzymes and proliferation, linking membrane signaling and mitochondrial calcium handling to T-cell metabolic and transcriptional programs.",
